---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import CalloutBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/CalloutBuild.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="callout-relative-colors">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A callout with severities needs a background, border, text and icon color
      for each one, in light and in dark. Five severities times four colors
      times two schemes is 40 hand-picked values, plus a media query or theme
      class to switch between them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Add a severity or tweak a hue and you pick them all again. Preprocessors
      could compute shades, but only at build time, not from a custom property.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Each severity class sets a single <code>--palette-source</code> color
      </li>
      <li>
        The palette derives 16 shades from it with <code>oklch(from …)</code>: a
        fixed lightness per step, chroma scaled from the source, the hue nudged
        a degree per step
      </li>
      <li>
        The callout picks shades with <code>light-dark()</code>, so dark mode
        needs no media query
      </li>
      <li>
        The tint sits on a translucent <code>::before</code> layer over the opaque
        surface
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.info {
  --palette-source: oklch(0.58 0.21 var(--hue-blue));
  --color-2: oklch(from var(--palette-source) 97% calc(0.06 * c / 0.21) h);
  --color-5: oklch(from var(--palette-source) 80% calc(0.16 * c / 0.21) h);
  --color-9: oklch(from var(--palette-source) 53% calc(0.2 * c / 0.21) h);
}

.callout.info {
  --bg-color: light-dark(
    oklch(from var(--color-2) l c h / 20%),
    oklch(from var(--color-6) l c h / 5%)
  );
  --border-color: light-dark(var(--color-5), var(--color-11));
  --icon-color: var(--color-9);
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <CalloutBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        There's no <code>@supports</code> fallback. The whole palette, grays included,
        is built from relative colors
      </li>
      <li>
        Without relative colors or <code>light-dark()</code>, the declarations
        that use them are invalid at computed-value time and fall back to their
        initial or inherited values, so the theme loses its colors, not just the
        callout
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
